  the scroll of the sourceless number (source)
  A ninja knows where each of his numbers came from.

    /dojo-elsewhere/moved.y:6  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.
    temple/bells.go:10  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.
    temple/blade.go:25  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.
    temple/outside_test.go:47  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.
    temple/temple.go:17  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.
    temple/universe.go:9  A number named here says why, but not that it is defined; measured or picked, it lives in data.

  A measured number goes in the research data, pulled from its source; a
  picked number goes in the picked numbers, with who chose it. Code asks
  for both by name. A number true by definition stays, and its comment
  says it is defined.

  the scroll of the defensive feint (feint)
  A ninja who is refused leaves no feint behind, for whoever finds an
  empty blade will take it for a real one.

    temple/feint.go:11  feintThing{} is an empty value handed back with an error. A caller may use it unchecked. On a refusal return nothing usable: a pointer, nil; a slice, nil; or change the result.
    temple/feint.go:16  made is a feintThing declared and never set, handed back with an error. A caller may use it unchecked. On a refusal return nothing usable: a pointer, nil; a slice, nil; or change the result.
    temple/feint.go:20  feintThing{...} has fields set and is handed back with an error. It looks like real data, so a caller who skips the check uses it. Check the error first and build only on success: if err != nil { return nil, err }. On a refusal return nothing usable: a pointer, nil; a slice, nil; or change the result.

  On a refusal return nothing usable: a pointer, nil; a slice, nil; or
  change the result so that no value comes back beside the error.

  the scroll of the empty vow (vow)
  A vow without words is no vow.

    /dojo-elsewhere/generated.y:3  The vow to break stranger covers nothing here. Put it down.
    temple/bare.go:3  The vow names "", which is no scroll.
    temple/nowhere.go:1  The vow to break stranger covers nothing here. Put it down.
    temple/universe.go:14  The vow names "nosuch", which is no scroll.
    temple/vows.go:10  The vow to break name covers nothing here. Put it down.

  Name the scroll you break and say why, on the vow's own line.
  00:14:33 "Promise me you will never forget."

  the scroll of the crowded vows (crowd)
  A ninja who vows at every step has stopped training.

    /dojo-elsewhere/generated.y:3  The module carries 14 vows over 16 tests, more than one in 20. Bring your vows to the master.

  A ninja has pride and does not run from a fight. Where a scroll itself
  is wrong, bring it to the master, so it can be rewritten. A vow does
  not pay for a vow.

Understand?

village, stay here. Sit down.

  the scroll of the stranger's house (stranger)
  A ninja does not build in another's house.

    village/alias.go:10  Builds temple.Sword by literal.
    village/village.go:10  Builds temple.Sword by literal.

  Ask the package that owns the type for one, through its constructor,
  and let it refuse what it cannot be.
  00:14:29 "This is my fate, and mine alone."

  the scroll of wasted power (waste)
  A ninja shows only the weapons he must carry.

    village/nameless.go:4  Twice is exported, and nothing outside village uses it.
    village/place.go:5  Head is exported, and nothing outside village uses it.

  No one outside the package calls it. Make it private.
  00:39:25 "He must never waste his powers."

  the scroll of the practice sword (practice)
  A ninja trains with the weapons he will carry, and no others.

    village/leak.go:3  fmttest is made for tests, and real code imports it.

  Only tests call it from outside. Make it private, and test through
  what the package offers. If that is hard, the difficulty is the
  lesson: it is telling you the interface. A package named for its
  tests, like httptest, is no practice sword: only tests may import it,
  and some test must.
  00:12:37 "Master, it's practice time."

  the scroll of the borrowed blade (blade)
  A ninja forges a blade once.

    village/first.go:5  temple.Hone(temple.Forge(...)) is forged here, and again at village/second.go:5, village/third.go:5.
    village/loud1.go:11  temple.Tie(strings.ToUpper(...)) is forged here, and again at village/loud2.go:10, village/loud3.go:10.
    village/loud2.go:10  temple.Tie(strings.ToUpper(...)) is forged here, and again at village/loud1.go:11, village/loud3.go:10.
    village/loud3.go:10  temple.Tie(strings.ToUpper(...)) is forged here, and again at village/loud1.go:11, village/loud2.go:10.
    village/second.go:5  temple.Hone(temple.Forge(...)) is forged here, and again at village/first.go:5, village/third.go:5.
    village/third.go:5  temple.Hone(temple.Forge(...)) is forged here, and again at village/first.go:5, village/second.go:5.

  Find what each place needs, build it once where it belongs, and have
  each place ask for it. Then put the copies down.
  00:39:08 "Violence is not the only way to solve a problem for a ninja.
  Patience is sometimes even stronger."

  the scroll of the buried path (buried)
  A ninja does not bury his things where only he can dig.

    village/bury.go:7  "/tmp/dojo.txt" is one machine's path, handed to os.WriteFile.

  A path written into the code is true on one machine. Take it from the
  config, or from the directory the tool is given.

  the scroll of the nameless blade (literal)
  A ninja names every blade he carries.

    village/nameless.go:6  A function is written here with no name.

  Give it a name: a function, or a method on a type that holds what it
  needs. A goroutine runs a named method; an api that takes a function
  is handed a named one.

  the scroll of the numbered place (index)
  A ninja knows what is kept in each place, not where it is.

    village/place.go:6  [0] says a place, not what is there.
    village/place.go:6  [1] says a place, not what is there.

  Use the stack's own ways for a stack, a struct with named fields for a
  fixed set of parts, and where a place must be named, declare it once,
  with why.

  the scroll of the bare number (number)
  A ninja says what each number is and where it came from.

    village/alias.go:10  3 is a bare number.
    village/belts.go:7  1 is a bare number.
    village/drum_test.go:14  0 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:17  3 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:17  3 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:23  0 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:24  0 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:25  2 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:27  10 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:28  3 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:36  0 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:37  0 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:38  2 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:40  10 is a bare number.
    village/more_test.go:41  3 is a bare number.
    village/nameless.go:5  0 is a bare number.
    village/village_test.go:11  2 is a bare number.

  Name it in a declaration that says why, or say what it does with a
  small named function written once: rest(share), not 1 - share;
  positive(x), not x > 0. 0 and 1 are numbers too.

  the scroll of the empty vow (vow)
  A vow without words is no vow.

    village/village.go:9  The vow to break stranger gives no reason.

  Name the scroll you break and say why, on the vow's own line.
  00:14:33 "Promise me you will never forget."

Understand?

